• Not Loving Enough (Spiritual Numbness): Sloth. Losing enthusiasm for the people and things one should care about—what modern people call "languishing" or "lying flat" [01:2, 01:145, 01:191]. (This corresponds to Ignorance/Delusion in Buddhism, where the mind dies a little first) [01:168, 01:341].
• Loving Too Excessively (Overstepping Boundaries): Greed, Gluttony, Lust. Infinitely consuming external materials or sensory pleasures to fill an inner void [01:206, 01:255]. (This corresponds to Greed/Attachment in Buddhism) [01:341].

2. The Intersection of East and West: The Seven Sins vs. The Three Poisons & Self-Grasping

The video highlights that 800 years before Western monks began observing the human mind, spiritual practitioners along the Ganges (Buddhists) identified the root causes of psychological suffering as the "Three Poisons": Greed, Anger, and Ignorance [01:334, 01:337]. Digging even deeper, only one thing remains: "Self-Grasping" (the Ego) [01:342].
Whether it is the "I am higher than you" of Pride, the "I am lower than you" of Envy, or the "I want more" of Greed, the subject of all seven afflictions is always "I" [01:135, 01:326, 01:330]. This aligns perfectly with the Buddhist verse: "All the evil karma I have created in the past, born of my body, speech, and mind, all stems from beginningless greed, anger, and ignorance."

3. The Ultimate Antidote: Not Suppression, but "Seeing"

The video concludes with a brilliant antidote—true transformation does not mean forcefully suppressing a thought, but rather stripping away its power to make decisions for you [01:356, 01:362].
The moment a desire, anger, or numbness arises, if you can step back instead of getting dragged into it, you "see" it clearly like a mirror: "Oh, Wrath is here again" [01:368].
• In the West: This is symbolized by climbing the mountain of Purgatory and having the letters of sin wiped from your forehead [01:13].
